If I had to offer a comparison, what it basically boils down to, is
that the nethack "dev team" has made a lot of choices which give the
game a particular flavor; and thats fine, but it's much more
reflective of their personal taste than something which would appeal
to a large number of people. Nethack is a bit of a niche game, it
isn't very popular; and that is what is ultimately used as a
justification for not catering to what would otherwise be "popular
opinion".

Slashem, as with several other variations, are basically "better and
more fun", in that they make much more sense to readers of what might
otherwise be termed "modern" and "popular" fantasy... Tolkein,
Dragonlance, Pern, WoW series, Diablo, star trek, douglass, et cetera.
The way it is now, Nethack is a mishmash of odd, out of sight fantasy
that no one remembers anymore. For example the whole wumpus thing. Do
you know what a wumpus is? Actually I do, because i played it on a
mainframe in the early eighties - and on my vic 20 (or was that
commodore 64? or both?) - but hey, you can't have everything. If you
could, there would be a lot more infocom (trinity, for example) and
bard's tale references in nethack.

Well, again, slashem is cool, but I'm working on vanilla nethack
first. Just so I can put it down once and for all. And I will, since
I've played slashem and it is superior. Look, I just want to play a
lawful elf, okay? Whats your fucking problem? And maybe I want to play
a hobbit, or a grey elf, okay? It's what *I WANT*. It doesn't break
the fucking game. And stuff like ice mage, fire mage, is just cool.
It's refreshing. It ISN'T worthless and ISN'T boring or dead-
end.Variety is the spice of life. Personally I see the LACK of options
as a design flaw; no time was devoted to allowing the vanilla game to
handle those other options... and the "dev team" closed their minds to
so-called "outside help"... the whole situation reeks of incompetence,
in many ways... *shrug*

